clearpolitics.com\/epolls\/2016\/governor\/nh\/new_hampshire_governor_sununu_vs_van_ostern-5411.html\">Governor<\/a><span><span>\u00a0<\/span><\/span>|<span>\u00a0<\/span><a href=\"https:\/\/www.realclearpolitics.com\/epolls\/2016\/senate\/nh\/new_hampshire_senate_ayotte_vs_hassan-3862.html\">Senate<\/a><span><span>\u00a0<\/span><\/span>|<span>\u00a0<\/span><a href=\"http:\/\/www.realclearpolitics.com\/epolls\/2016\/house\/nh\/new_hampshire_1st_district_guinta_vs_sheaporter-6018.html\">NH-1<\/a><strong><br \/>2014<\/strong>:<span><span>\u00a0<\/span><\/span><a href=\"http:\/\/www.realclearpolitics.com\/epolls\/2014\/governor\/nh\/new_hampshire_governor_havenstein_vs_hassan-5016.html\">Governor<\/a><span><span>\u00a0<\/span><\/span>|<span><span>\u00a0<\/span><\/span><a href=\"http:\/\/www.realclearpolitics.com\/epolls\/2014\/house\/nh\/new_hampshire_1st_district_guinta_vs_sheaporter-4142.html\">NH-1<\/a><span><span>\u00a0<\/span><\/span>|<span><span>\u00a0<\/span><\/span><a href=\"http:\/\/www.realclearpolitics.com\/epolls\/2014\/house\/nh\/new_hampshire_2nd_district_garcia_vs_kuster-4289.html\">NH-2<\/a><strong><br \/>2012<\/strong>:<span>\u00a0<\/span><a href=\"http:\/\/www.realclearpolitics.com\/epolls\/2012\/president\/nh\/new_hampshire_romney_vs_obama-2030.html\">President<\/a><span>\u00a0<\/span>|<span><span>\u00a0<\/span><\/span><a href=\"http:\/\/www.realclearpolitics.com\/epolls\/2012\/governor\/nh\/new_hampshire_governor_lamontagne_vs_hassan-2804.html\">Governor<\/a><span><span>\u00a0<\/span><\/span>|<span>\u00a0<\/span><a href=\"http:\/\/www.realclearpolitics.com\/epolls\/2012\/house\/nh\/new_hampshire_1st_district_guinta_vs_sheaporter-2814.html\">NH-1<\/a><span>\u00a0<\/span>|<span>\u00a0<\/span><a href=\"http:\/\/www.realclearpolitics.com\/epolls\/2012\/house\/nh\/new_hampshire_2nd_district_bass_vs_kuster-2815.html\">NH-2<\/a><\/p>"},"dataAnalysis":{"title":"Race Analysis","content":"<p>For most of the late 20th century, New Hampshire\u2019s politics were driven by an influx of citizens from Massachusetts. In the 1970s and \u201980s, these voters sympathized with the taxpayer revolt and threw out both Democratic senators in favor of Republicans. In the 1990s and 2000s, these same voters, like many Northern suburbanites, began to rebel against the Southern-accented GOP\u2019s conservative values on social issues, and the state elected mostly Democratic governors, while Republicans saw their margins in the Senate races shrink. In 2008, they lost one of their Senate seats when a scion of a prominent New Hampshire Republican family was handily defeated by former Democratic governor Jeanne Shaheen.<\/p>\n<p>Now Shaheen is retiring, and Republicans see the seat as their best shot at a Senate pickup this cycle. They\u2019ve nominated John E. Sununu, the man that Shaheen beat in 2008. He\u2019s also the brother of former governor Chris Sununu, who has remained very popular in the state. In the primary, Sununu dispatched former Sen. Scott Brown. He did so with President Trump\u2019s endorsement despite once calling Trump a \u201closer.\u201d Democrats nominated Rep. Chris Pappas, a four-term congressman from the 1st District. Sununu is probably the best candidate Republicans could have gotten, and the name still means something here, but he hasn't won a race since 2002, and he is running in a midterm with an unpopular Republican president in the White House. Polls currently show a close race. Given the environment, Pappas probably pulls it out, but Sununu is exactly the sort of candidate who can outrun a Democratic wave.<\/p>"},"showLineChart":1,"showBarChart":1,"showLineChartFirst":0,"sideCharts":{"charts_on_top":0,"left_chart_race_id":"7230","right_chart_race_id":"3894"},"show_final_results":0,"chart_data":{"chart_id":"7402","chart_axis_x":"1","chart_grid_x":"4","chart_axis_y":"1","chart_grid_y":"10","chart_min":"","chart_max":"","chart_start":"1772344800","chart_end":"","poll_id":"8845","chart_highlight":"#bbbb00","chart